Make Create and Update verbs over the Latent shape, then move the Editor's judgment into Editor-domain rules

Two increments, 2026-10-14 and 2026-10-15, in one commit because the shared documents hold
both sets of edits and Dev's run on the first had not been pasted when the second began.

ONE (2026-10-14). Dev asked why User had Read and Delete but not Create and Update. The
honest answer was history: Latent was named for what it carries before the CRUD view, and
Create/Update were mapped onto it with the ontology deciding by identity — so a Create of an
existing name was a silent update and an Update of a misspelled name a silent creation. Dev
accepted "Create and Update deriving Latent". `Latent` is the abstract candidate SHAPE
(definition, members; no factory; from Request); `Create` and `Update` derive it with
factories; User holds Create, Delete, Latent, Read, Request, Response, Update. Latent has no
codec now, so the initialization client's `domains`, `order`, `ordered` and `run` are typed
by Create (it builds from an empty ontology).

TWO (2026-10-15). Dev observed that the dreams domains carried no rule aspects, only an
actor firing rules — all five of them core's. True, and the Editor had meanwhile grown
judgment of its own in actor code: the intent checks as `holder` lookups folded inside four
case branches, and the whole-ontology judgment as a global building a session. That
contradicts the framework's own rule that an actor is a thin membrane. Dev accepted
"Editor-domain rules with the actor thinned".

- draco.DomainOntology gains `holds(typeName)` (a domain of that identity, name and
  package, parameters free) and `holding(typeName)` (the same as a Presence) — the lookup
  the Editor's `holder` global had, now the ontology's.
- User's `Request` declares three bodiless dyns every verb implements: `proposal(accepted)`,
  the ontology the request asks for (Create/Update: `including` the candidate; Delete:
  `excluding`; Read: the accepted ontology itself); `subject(accepted)` and
  `contents(accepted)`, the definition and members the Actual reports on (Create/Update:
  their own; Read/Delete: the held domain, or a stub and nothing). A request knows what it
  proposes; the Editor needs no dispatch of its own.
- Editor gains `Accepted` (the accepted ontology as a FACT, distinct from the raw
  provisional `DomainOntology` the core rules join — one DomainOntology fact in working
  memory, never two) and four RULES: `CreateHeld` (var c Create, var a Accepted; con
  a.ontology.holds(identity) → Problem "already in the accepted ontology"),
  `UpdateUnheld`, `ReadUnheld`, `DeleteUnheld` (the negation, "not in the accepted
  ontology"). The dreams layer now states its editing knowledge as definitions.
- The Editor actor is the membrane: nine patterns build its knowledge (five core, four
  Editor); per message it binds `provisional = msg.proposal(accepted)`, `subject`,
  `contents` and `changing = provisional != accepted` STRICTLY (`now fix`, before the state
  swap — a lazy binding would read the swapped state), inserts the request, `Accepted(accepted)`,
  the provisional's domains and definitions and the provisional itself, fires, closes, swaps
  the state only when quiet, delivers `Actual(subject, contents, problems)` and the ontology
  only on acceptance of a change. No case branches, no globals, no judgment: which rule
  matches is the dispatch.
- ENGINE: codec eligibility is TRANSITIVE. `Accepted(ontology: DomainOntology)` would have
  emitted an uncompilable codec (DRACO.md §6, "codec eligibility is not transitive").
  `DracoGenerator.namesCodecless` now loads a factory parameter's draco type and asks
  `hasCodec` of it with its LOADED parent chain as family context, so a family member still
  finds its discriminated parent and a child its inherited elements; a stub (host primitive,
  scenario type) stays with the text rule; a cycle is broken optimistically. DracoGenTest is
  byte-identical over the main corpus (319 / 0 with the gates and rule suites), so no existing
  type changed its codec — as expected, since an uncompilable codec could not have been there.

